        <!-- Article Body -->
        <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">A battery energy storage system can meet every harmonic limit at its point of common coupling and still introduce a resonant frequency into the network. Compliance confirms that voltage and current distortion stayed inside defined limits under the conditions assessed. Resonance describes how the whole electrical system behaves across frequency, and it does not appear on a compliance certificate. This article covers the mechanism, what it costs, why fixed filters fall short, and how an <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">LV AHF + SVG</strong> pair handles power quality problems at the source.</p>
<h2 id="what-bess-harmonic-resonance-actually-is" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">What BESS Harmonic Resonance Actually Is</h2>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">Resonance arises when the inductive and capacitive elements of a grid-connected BESS interact at a specific frequency. A utility-scale plant is more than a bank of cells: its AC connection includes power conversion systems, inverter output filters, step-up transformers, medium-voltage collector cables, grid-connection transformers, auxiliary transformers, reactive power equipment, and protection and switching gear.</p>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">Transformers and the external network supply inductance. Cables, inverter filters and capacitor banks supply capacitance. At a given frequency those reactances combine into either a <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">parallel</strong> or a <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">series</strong> resonance.</p>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6"><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">Parallel resonance</strong> — high network impedance at one frequency. A modest harmonic current there produces a disproportionately large harmonic voltage at the PCC.</p>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6"><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">Series resonance</strong> — a low-impedance path at one frequency. Excess harmonic current circulates through transformers, cables and capacitors, raising heating and dielectric stress even when the PCC voltage looks acceptable. Both voltage and current must be measured.</p>
<h2 id="why-grid-compliance-does-not-prove-absence-of-resonance" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">Why Grid Compliance Does Not Prove Absence of Resonance</h2>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">Grid codes and harmonic standards set voltage and current distortion limits at the PCC. Passing them shows the measured or calculated results satisfy those limits — for the operating conditions, network configuration and assumptions built into the assessment. Resonance is a property of the complete system impedance, so it can sit in the network while distortion stays below the limit, visible only when enough harmonic energy exists near that frequency.</p>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">A BESS may therefore pass its initial assessment and run into trouble later after the short-circuit level changes, a transformer is taken out of service, a cable circuit is disconnected, a capacitor bank is switched, another renewable plant connects nearby, background distortion rises, the BESS changes operating mode, or the number of energized inverters changes.</p>
<h2 id="how-a-bess-reshapes-network-impedance" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">How a BESS Reshapes Network Impedance</h2>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">A conventional harmonic study models the inverter as a current source. That misses the inverter's frequency-dependent output impedance, shaped by current-control bandwidth, PLL settings, digital control delays, active damping, voltage feed-forward, switching frequency, output filter design, active and reactive power, and grid-following versus grid-forming control. At some frequencies the inverter looks inductive; at others, capacitive.</p>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">The governing relationship is simple:</p>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">Harmonic voltage = harmonic impedance × harmonic current</p>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">Where network impedance is low, a given harmonic current produces limited voltage distortion. Where impedance rises sharply near a parallel resonance, the same current produces a far higher harmonic voltage. Distortion is governed by the impedance the current flows through, not only by what the inverter emits.</p>
<h2 id="the-role-of-medium-voltage-cables" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">The Role of Medium-Voltage Cables</h2>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">MV BESS plants often run extensive cable networks linking inverter stations to a common switchboard. That cable capacitance, combined with transformer and grid inductance, can create a parallel resonant point. More cable raises total capacitance, which pushes resonance toward a lower frequency and closer to harmonic components from the inverters or already present in the grid.</p>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">The final resonant frequency depends on cable length and construction, the number of parallel circuits, transformer leakage impedance, grid short-circuit level, the number of connected inverters, and existing filters and capacitor banks. Treating the collector system as a fundamental-frequency impedance hides this behaviour.</p>
<h2 id="individually-compliant-inverters-can-still-interact" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">Individually Compliant Inverters Can Still Interact</h2>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">A utility-scale BESS may contain tens or hundreds of inverter modules in parallel. Each may satisfy its equipment-level harmonic requirements against a specified reference impedance, yet connecting many to a common collector alters the plant's equivalent impedance. The harmonic current seen at one inverter includes its own emission, current from other parallel inverters, current driven by background grid voltage distortion, and circulating current between units. Each inverter and output filter also adds a resonant peak, and the combined response grows more complex as units come online.</p>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">Twenty individually compliant inverters are not electrically equivalent to one inverter multiplied by twenty. The full plant must be studied as an interconnected system.</p>
<h2 id="lessons-from-a-50-mw-bess-study" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">Lessons from a 50 MW BESS Study</h2>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">A study examined a 50 MW BESS on a 33 kV network with 19 inverters and their step-up transformers. Commissioning and operating measurements revealed harmonic non-compliance across multiple charge and discharge conditions, spanning roughly the <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">14th to 65th</strong> harmonic orders. Two resonant regions were identified: one between approximately the <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">14th and 20th</strong> orders tied to the self-impedance of the BESS inverters, and one between approximately the <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">50th and 70th</strong> orders linked to the capacitance of the 33 kV collector cables.</p>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">Inverter emissions alone could not explain the problem — the BESS had altered network impedance and amplified particular components. A validated system model built from idle, charging and discharging measurements led to a <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">5 MVAr C-type harmonic filter</strong> on the 33 kV BESS switchboard, which damped both regions and brought the installation within the applicable limits.</p>
<h2 id="why-full-power-is-not-always-the-worst-case" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">Why Full Power Is Not Always the Worst Case</h2>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">Maximum charge or discharge is often assumed to yield the highest distortion. It does not always. At low output, fewer inverter modules run, harmonic cancellation between units decreases, current distortion as a percentage of fundamental can rise, control modes may change, plant impedance may shift, and background voltage distortion can dominate. The most severe resonance can occur during partial charging, partial discharging, reactive support or standby. An assessment based only on rated active power can miss the worst case.</p>
<h2 id="how-network-conditions-shift-the-resonant-frequency" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">How Network Conditions Shift the Resonant Frequency</h2>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">Network impedance is not fixed. A strong grid with multiple transformers and circuits in service has a different harmonic impedance profile from a weak grid during a planned outage, and changes in inductance move the resonant frequency toward or away from significant harmonic sources. Relevant conditions include minimum and maximum short-circuit levels, normal and outage configurations, transformer availability, bus-section switching, tap positions, nearby generation status, capacitor and reactor switching, and future reinforcements. A single short-circuit level and X/R ratio cannot represent the external grid — use an impedance envelope or multiple frequency-dependent cases.</p>
<h2 id="mitigation-passive-active-and-the-case-for-ahf-svg" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">Mitigation: Passive, Active, and the Case for AHF + SVG</h2>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">The right method depends on frequency range, network impedance, inverter behaviour and reactive power requirement. Passive filters, C-type filters and damped high-pass filters give a low-impedance path for selected orders and add damping, and remain economical at medium voltage and large ratings. But every passive filter changes the network impedance; a poorly designed one moves an existing resonant point or creates a new one.</p>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">An <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">active harmonic filter</strong> measures harmonic current or voltage and injects a compensating signal, adapting as conditions change. A <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">static var generator</strong> injects or absorbs reactive current per phase to hold voltage and power factor. For harmonics mitigation in a PV plant, the AHF removes the 5th, 7th, 11th and 13th distortion that nonlinear loads and inverters add, while the SVG supplies continuous reactive support and damping margin. CHITEK AHF and CHITEK SVG are built for exactly this system-level role.</p>
<div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6"><table class="w-full border-collapse"><thead><tr class="bg-gray-50"><th class="text-left p-4 font-semibold text-text-dark border-b border-border-light">Capability</th><th class="text-left p-4 font-semibold text-text-dark border-b border-border-light">Fixed bank</th><th class="text-left p-4 font-semibold text-text-dark border-b border-border-light">Hybrid TSC + SVG</th><th class="text-left p-4 font-semibold text-text-dark border-b border-border-light">Active SVG (3-level)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">Response</td><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">Seconds, stepped</td><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">Fast, dynamic</td><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">Within one grid cycle</td></tr><tr><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">Reactive range</td><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">Capacitive only</td><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">Capacitive + trim</td><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">Continuous, leading and lagging</td></tr><tr><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">Harmonics</td><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">None; can resonate</td><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">Partial</td><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">97% reduction; THDi ≤ 5%</td></tr><tr><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">Phase imbalance</td><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">Not addressed</td><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">Partial</td><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">Independent per phase</td></tr><tr><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">Resonance risk</td><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">High</td><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">Managed</td><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">Low, actively coordinated</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h2 id="igbt-vs-sic" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">IGBT vs SiC</h2>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">Conventional LV AHF and SVG units use IGBTs capped near <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">150 °C</strong>, switching typically below <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">20 kHz</strong>. SiC MOSFETs run at a <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">200 °C</strong> junction, carry roughly <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">10×</strong> the breakdown field, switch at <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">2–3×</strong> the frequency, and cut switching loss by about <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">50%</strong>. That wider control bandwidth lets the converter track fast harmonic and reactive transients and shrinks the cabinet. CHITEK's LV AHF + SVG platform is built on this direction.</p>
<h2 id="engineering-deployment-checklist" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">Engineering Deployment Checklist</h2>
<ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-2 mb-6 text-text-mid leading-relaxed"><li>Run frequency-dependent impedance scans across the range required by the grid code; identify parallel peaks, series troughs and how they move between cases.</li><li>Study multiple BESS conditions: full and partial charge, full and partial discharge, standby, zero-active-power reactive support, and reduced inverter availability.</li><li>Use accurate component data — transformer impedance, cable capacitance, filter parameters. Small errors in capacitance or inductance shift the calculated resonant frequency.</li><li>Obtain frequency-dependent inverter models, or black-box Norton/Thévenin equivalents where control models cannot be shared.</li><li>Measure background voltage distortion before energizing the BESS to separate new emissions from amplified existing distortion.</li><li>Validate the model against post-commissioning measurements; correct it before sizing any mitigation equipment.</li><li>Design the filter against the full impedance profile, credible contingencies, component tolerances and future harmonic sources — not against the largest order in one report.</li></ol>
<h2 id="typical-application-profiles" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">Typical Application Profiles</h2>
<ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 mb-6 text-text-mid leading-relaxed"><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">Utility-scale BESS on MV collector networks</strong> — cable capacitance pushes resonance low; AHF + SVG damp it while meeting reactive requirements.</li><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">Hybrid PV + storage plants</strong> — harmonics from PV inverters stack with BESS emissions; a combined AHF + SVG handles both.</li><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">Weak-grid renewable connections</strong> — low short-circuit level raises impedance sensitivity; the SVG holds voltage and the AHF tracks distortion.</li><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">Plants with existing capacitor banks</strong> — banks shift resonance; audit them inside the model before commissioning any new filter.</li></ul>
<h2 id="bottom-line" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">Bottom Line</h2>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">BESS harmonic resonance is a system interaction problem, not an inverter emission problem, and compliance today does not describe the resonance tomorrow. A validated impedance study plus an <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">LV AHF + SVG</strong> pair gives continuous, adaptive damping where fixed filters would only move the peak. CHITEK's AHF and SVG platforms are specified from exactly this measurement-first discipline.</p>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">Where are the resonant peaks on your collector network — and what will excite them at part load?</p>
